<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrés Avelino Cáceres</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrés Avelino <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> was a Peruvian general who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance against the Chilean occupation in the central highlands during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, earning the name the Wizard of the Andes. He was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from 1886 to 1890 and from 1894 to 1895, presiding over postwar reconstruction and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grace-contract">Grace Contract</a>. A coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-pierola">Nicolás de Piérola</a> overthrew him in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andres-avelino-caceres.jpg" alt="Andrés Avelino Cáceres" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugenio Courret · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9s_Avelino_C%C3%A1ceres_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrés Avelino Cáceres Dorregaray was born on 10 November in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>. His father, Domingo Cáceres y Ore, was a landowner, and his mother, Justa Dorregaray Cueva, was the daughter of a Spanish colonel. He was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> and counted the Inka-Wanka princess Catalina Huanca among his maternal ancestors. In 1854 he left his studies to join the Ayacucho Battalion as a cadet, and he fought in the rebellion of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla">Ramón Castilla</a> at the Battle of La Palma on 5 January 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> for criticising President Juan Antonio Pezet, who had allowed the Spanish occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands">Chincha Islands</a>, and he fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-callao">Battle of Callao</a> on 2 May 1866. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, which began on 5 April 1879, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a>, where his intervention was decisive in a Peruvian victory. He was at the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> on 26 May 1880 and was wounded at Miraflores in the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. When Lima fell in January 1881 he escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jauja">Jauja</a>.</p><p>Named political and military chief of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> on 26 April 1881, he organised a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war that mobilised the peasant population. He defeated several Chilean expeditions, at Pucará, Marcavalle and La Concepción, and was nicknamed the Brujo de los Andes. The better armed Chilean forces defeated him at Huamachuco on 10 July 1883, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon">Treaty of Ancón</a> of 10 October 1883 ended the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> refused to recognise President Miguel Iglesias, and in the civil war that followed he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 28 November 1885 and forced Iglesias to resign on 12 December. Elected as the sole candidate, he took office on 3 June 1886. His government signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grace-contract">Grace Contract</a> on 28 October 1888, which handed the railways, a guano concession and annual payments over 33 years to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s creditors in exchange for paying the debt and expanding the railways. Critics accused his administration of selling the country's main assets cheaply.</p><p>He handed power to Remigio Morales on 10 August 1890, and began a second term on 10 August 1894 amid accusations of fraud. Rebellions united under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-pierola">Nicolás de Piérola</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 17 March 1895, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caceres">Cáceres</a> resigned after an armistice. He later served as ambassador in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, became a Marshal on 10 November 1919, and died at Ancón on 10 October 1923, aged 86.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrés Avelino Cáceres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chile took Tarapacá, Tacna and Arica and occupied Lima in January 1881, while <b>Andrés Avelino Cáceres</b> led guerrilla resistance in the central highlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.16,-74.22&amp;year=1892" aria-label="Ayacucho on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2514 2654 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Andrés Avelino Cáceres"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2964 2924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2964 2924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.16,-74.22&amp;year=1892">What was here in 1892? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
